WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.570
Today is all about Seedance 2.5 and
getting AI video that looks and

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.120
feels like real footage. Start with
characters and a location, then bring

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.560
them together in one cinematic scene.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.890
Direct the action, shape the camera
movement, and bring emotion to every

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.910
performance. Use your references to show
the model what matters.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.230
Then describe the scene you want to
create.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.560
And if the result isn't quite right,
refine your prompt and try another

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:34.070
take. Let's build the scene together.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.840
Hi, I'm Adil. If you've ever watched an AI
video and thought, "How did they

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.260
get it to do that?" That's what we're
going to figure out together.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.320
Think of this as your first day on set.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.030
You're directing, I'll help you get your
idea onto the screen.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.250
Open Cinema Studio and go back to the
project we used in Lesson 4.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.540
Our characters, locations, and props are
already here, so we can pick up

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.400
right where we left off. First, create a
new folder called Generations and

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.840
open it. Here, we'll write prompts for
video generations.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.090
Switch to Video, open the Model menu, and
choose Seedance

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.080
2.5. You've already used it in the
previous lessons.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.240
Now, let's get to know it better.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.680
Let's bring in our biker from Lesson 4.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.020
We can drag his image straight into the
prompt box and refer to it as Image

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.410
1.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.760
Or, we can save it as an element.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.760
An element saves an asset under a name.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.340
A quick @ mention brings its saved
references into the prompt, so we don't

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.230
have to find and attach the same image for
every generation.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.340
Click the three dots, then choose Create
Element and Image Element.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.540
Set the category to Character and give it
a simple name, Biker.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.320
The category adds the right prefix to the
element ID automatically.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.360
Switch the category and you can see that
prefix change.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.260
We'll keep Character and click Create.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.560
Back in Generations, type the @ sign and
select Biker.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.230
I'll create elements for our other
references the same way.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.360
Our friend, the bar interior, the bar
entrance, and the

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.960
suitcase. And now, we're ready to write
the prompt.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.100
Now, it's your turn. Pause here and
prepare four or five elements from

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.340
Lesson 4 or the supplied references.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.920
Give each one a clear, different name.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.360
Type the @ sign to check the references.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.200
Come back to the lesson when you're done.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:46.450
With Seedance, you can bring a face, a
place, a camera move, and a voice

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.770
into the same generation. It works with
text, images, video, and audio

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.640
together. That's what multimodal means.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.670
The prompt is where you describe what
happens and what's visible in plain

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.780
language and give the model a guide on
which reference represents which

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.780
part. Seedance 2.5 can generate 4 to 30
seconds of video with sound, either

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.220
as one continuous shot or as a sequence of
shots with cuts.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.100
It's called multi-shot. Seedance chooses
the shot structure based on your

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.720
prompt. For a single shot video, describe
one continuous take.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.520
For multi-shot, describe each shot and
where you want the cuts.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.120
Image references help you specify what
things look like.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.200
Each character image gives us that
person's face and clothes.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.230
The prop image gives us the object shape,
color, and details.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.220
Our location image gives us the space,
light, and color.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.500
That's why we spent time creating those
assets in lesson 4 and why we're

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.740
choosing a clear reference for our prop.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:52.210
You can combine up to 30 images, 10
videos, and 10 audio files, 50

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.770
references in total. In our experience, up
to 12 images works

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.980
best. For our first scene, we'll use 5
images, 2 characters, 1

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.230
prop, and 2 views of our location.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.300
And after that, we give every reference a
clear job.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.340
Say which image defines each character,
which defines the prop, which

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.220
defines the location, and which clip is
there for the camera movement.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.400
That's how the references and the prompt
work together.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.040
We show sedance the details we care about,
then describe what happens.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.480
We'll start with images and build from
there.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.230
There's no single right way to do this.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.310
Sometimes I use several images of the same
character from different angles

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.300
or a separate reference just for the
outfit.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.750
Start with this structure, then play with
it until your results stay

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.090
consistent. Time to write a prompt and put
all of this to work.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.080
Before we write the prompt, let's go
through the settings first.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.460
Aspect ratio sets the frame shape.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.230
I'm using 21 by 9 for a cinematic look.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.810
Resolution controls the video size, and
duration is how many seconds sedance

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.500
generates. We'll set that once we've
planned the action.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.180
Turn audio on so sedance generates sound
with the video.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.600
Now, picture the scene you want to make.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.320
What's happening in it? That's where our
prompting begins.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.320
I want our biker to walk into the bar with
the suitcase, find his friend,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.170
and sit down beside him. We'll build the
prompt in three parts, scene

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.020
context, references, and shots.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.030
Scene context is the main visible action
across the whole clip in one or two

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.140
sentences. Here's ours. Scene context.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.100
Biker enters a bar with a suitcase looking
for his friend.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.840
They notice each other, and the biker
walks over and sits next to him.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.820
Next, references. We've described what
happens.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.460
Now we tell the model which image gives us
each part of the scene.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.230
This first one is our biker.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.620
I want to keep his face, hair, and
clothes, so I'll ask for him exactly as

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.120
shown. References. The biker, 100% as
shown in the reference image.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.780
His friend, 100% as shown in the reference
image.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.220
He is seated on a stool on the side of the
bar counter, directly in front of

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.320
the beer mug shown in location reference.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.820
Now, the suitcase. Its image gives us the
shape, color, and details.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.230
But we also need to say who carries it.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:19.840
It belongs to the biker, and he holds it
in his left hand until he puts it

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.260
on the counter. The suitcase, 100% as
shown in the reference image.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:27.920
It belongs to the biker, he carries it in
his left hand.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.940
This image gives us the bar, including its
light, color, and the people in

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:36.180
the background. I'll also keep the mug in
place so our friend has the same

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.500
seat throughout the scene.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.230
The bar interior, 100% as shown in the
reference image.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.340
Use it for the light, color, location, and
background people.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.980
The beer mug on the counter marks his
friend's seat.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.920
Keep the mug in its reference position.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.380
The bar entrance, entrance of the same
bar.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:58.140
Use it for the color and location, not for
composition or framing.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.040
That's what I mean by giving each
reference a job.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.960
If you only want someone's face, say that.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.550
Here, we're keeping the character's full
appearance.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.120
All right, so how do we want to film it?

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.520
Our scene, the shot size, controls how
much we see.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:18.220
A wide shot shows the character and their
surroundings.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.380
A close-up shows their expression.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:25.340
Download the shot size guide below this
video for visual examples to use

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.640
when you write your prompt.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.110
For the entrance, I want a medium close-up
from a low angle with the camera

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.140
rising to reveal his face.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.520
That upward camera move is called a jib
shot.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.230
We'll give it the first five seconds.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.340
Shot one, zero to five seconds, medium
close-up, low angle jib shot.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.980
Then we describe the action in order.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:49.920
Someone crosses in front of the camera,
the biker enters with the case, and

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.800
the camera rises as he looks for his
friend.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.580
I'll keep the case in his left hand, just
as we wrote in references.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.020
Now, I want to cut to the friend.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:05.230
We give that view a new shot number and a
time range, five to eight seconds.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.740
A medium shot leaves room for his gesture.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:12.480
The camera stays still while he turns and
raise a hand.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.020
Shot two, five to eight seconds.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.160
Keep the camera still at eye level.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:21.760
Show his friend from the waist up, from
behind and slightly to one side.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.100
He sits at the counter in front of the
beer mug.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.620
When he notices the biker, he turns
halfway towards him and raises a hand in

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.230
greeting. Back to the biker.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:36.040
A close-up catches his smile when he
recognizes his friend before he leaves

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:37.840
the frame towards the counter.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.420
Shot three, eight to eleven seconds.

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:44.360
Close-up at eye level, a three-quarter
view from the front.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.600
People pass around the biker and in front
of the camera.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.180
The camera holds as he recognizes his
friend and smiles.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.200
Then he exits from right to left.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:57.140
For the last shot, we're behind the
counter, looking at both of them.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.860
The biker sits beside his friend and puts
the case on the counter with his

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:04.800
left hand. We can put his line right into
the action.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.160
What's up? Then leave a little silence so
the clip has room to end.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.940
Shot four, eleven to sixteen seconds.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:15.570
Film from behind the bar, looking straight
at the characters at medium

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:19.880
distance. The biker enters from the right
and sits beside his friend, facing

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:24.110
the camera. He places the suitcase on the
counter with his left hand while

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.160
saying, "What's up?" to his friend.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.260
He releases the handle and settles
comfortably into his seat.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:33.900
Second of silence. I'll finish with a few
details that apply across the

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:38.920
whole clip. Our friend stays by the mug
and the bar stays busy.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:43.080
I also want depth, something near the
camera, the main action in the middle,

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:47.940
and the room behind it. For composition,
I'll ask for the rule of thirds,

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.160
placing important subjects along the lines
you get when you divide the frame

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.230
into thirds, horizontally and vertically.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:59.280
For details, his friend stays seated on
the customer side of the bar.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.200
The beer mug is directly in front of him.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.160
Keep that mug in the same place throughout
the scene.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.020
Give each shot depth. Put something near
the camera, something in the

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.540
middle, and something at the back.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:16.240
Use the rule of thirds. Keep the bar busy
and fun, like the reference.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.200
For sound, I want to hear the bar, his
footsteps, and his clothes as he

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:24.240
moves. If you've already written his
dialogue in shot four, I'll leave music

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.800
out. Natural bar ambience, footsteps, and
clothing movement.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.100
No music. I always read it once from top
to bottom.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.080
If I can picture the scene and I know who
does what and who's holding the

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:41.000
case, it's ready. Our last shot ends at 16
seconds, so that's the duration

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.450
we'll use for this generation.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.960
Alright, we've described the scene, let's
see how it plays.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.580
Before we generate, match the duration to
the timing in our prompt.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:57.160
16 seconds for us. If your last shot ends
at 12, set 12.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.620
Check the aspect ratio and resolution we
chose, keep audio on, and set the

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:05.240
generation count to 1. Here's the credit
cost for this take.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:07.280
Everything's set, let's generate.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.100
CDINs 2.5 also has a draft mode.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:16.210
It gives us a 480p preview, so we can
check how the scene plays

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.700
before spending credits on the final
render.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:23.860
Then we can turn our chosen take into a
1080p video.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:28.000
Draft mode is useful when you're testing
prompts or trying out ideas.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.340
You can try different versions using fewer
credits.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:35.680
Then upscale only the takes you like to
1080p.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:40.870
We use this workflow for complex scenes,
where it often takes a few attempts

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.000
to get the result we want.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.890
I'll be generating in 1080p throughout the
lesson, but you can follow along

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.500
in CDINs Draft. Now it's your turn.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:54.720
Pause here and write a simple scene with
your two characters, one prop, and

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.580
one location. Use the same three parts,
give each reference a clear role,

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.440
and name each actor in each shot.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.020
Then generate one take with your elements.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:08.230
Okay, our scene is ready. Let's check it
out.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.090
(footsteps)

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:26.480
What's up? Okay, yeah, that's pretty close
to what I had in

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:29.400
mind. Let's take a closer look.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.740
So after every generation, I ask three
simple questions.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:35.700
Who did what? Is it the right guy each
time?

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:39.380
Do I still recognize both of them, the
case and the bar?

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:42.230
And did the camera show me what I asked
for?

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:45.560
Spot where the problem happened, and fix
this one thing.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.640
The case is standing up here.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.680
I want it lying flat on the counter, ready
for him to open it.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.420
That's the part I'd change.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:57.660
Keep this take. We might use part of it
later in the edit.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:12:59.400
Watch the whole clip with sound.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.580
Choose one thing you want to change.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.020
If you already like the result, choose a
variation you'd like to try.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.620
Keep your prompt and your first take, then
come back.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.230
We'll use them to make the next decision.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.770
For our scene, I want the case flat on the
counter and a smaller reaction

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.120
when he spots his friend. I'm making two
small changes in one go here.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.860
When you're starting out, change one thing
at a time so you can see exactly

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:25.900
what it did. In scene context, I'll add
that he lays the case flat as he

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:30.520
sits down. In shot three, I'd like a small
nod instead of the smile.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.920
And in shot four, I'll be specific about
the position.

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.800
He lays the closed suitcase flat on the
counter in front of him with his

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.000
left hand, lit up, and handled toward him,
saying, "What's up?" to his

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.340
friend. With an action, I write it in
order.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.500
What happens first, what happens next?

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:48.700
With a camera move, I say where it starts
and what it follows.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:53.480
If a character, the prop, or the location
looks wrong, I go back to its

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:57.180
reference and to what I asked "see dance"
to take from it.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:01.250
And if the unwanted detail is sitting in
the image itself, I fix the asset

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.470
before I use it again. When the wrong guy
does the action, it's almost

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:10.020
always my wording. If I can't tell who he
is in a sentence, neither can "see

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.880
dance." With a prop, I also check where
the prompt puts it, who's supposed

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.440
to hold it, and whether that stays the
same through the whole scene.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.180
Then I read the rest of the prompt one
more time.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.450
Let's see what that changes.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.230
Hey! Hey!

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.360
What's up? Yeah, that's better.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.360
The case is flat on the counter now.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:45.560
This is a new take, so I watch the whole
scene again, not just the fix.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:49.290
I want the change I asked for, and I want
everything I liked in the first

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:50.960
version to still be there.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.480
I'm keeping the second take.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:55.860
The case is right in front of him, ready
for the next part of the scene.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.840
Now, try the change you picked earlier.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.230
Pause here, update the relevant
instruction, and generate one new take.

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:05.760
Compare it with your first version, and
choose the one you want to keep.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.900
You've now taken a scene from references
to a finished take.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.100
Describe, generate, review, and refine.

